 3. Exclusive: Engine update 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 The MV-75 Cheyenne, at an airport in Fort Worth, Texas. Photo: Al Drago/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Rolls-Royce brought its engine for the MV-75 Cheyenne to full power using a new digital controller known as a FADEC, executives told me.

 Why it matters: It's another milestone for this closely watched, multibillion-dollar tiltrotor project, which is expected to replace many of the U.S. Army's Black Hawk helicopters.

 What they're saying: "There are so many subsystems that go into a new aircraft design, and certification by the authorities," Scott Ames, a Rolls-Royce senior vice president, told me.

 "All of those have to line up and come together," he said. "The first time this configuration MV-75 lifts off the ground, you have to have checked all those boxes along the way." State of play: Each Cheyenne, built by Bell Textron , will be outfitted with two Rolls-Royce AE 1107 engines. (A different version can be found aboard the Bell-Boeing V-22 Osprey.)

 Engine testing kicked off in December in Indiana. The 101st Airborne Division at Fort Campbell, Kentucky, is expected to be the first to receive the tiltrotor. Zoom in: The FADEC used represents a "brand-new design" with "a lot of cyber-resilience features," according to Ames.

 Test results, he said, are shared "with Bell and the Army, to demonstrate that the AE 1107 meets all the requirements they're looking for." Go deeper: Vulcan supplying rare-earth magnets for Army drone push 

 
 
